(function(){window.PhysicaRankPyqNeet_zoology_bodyfluidsandcirculation=[{id:"neet-2026r-q112",pn:112,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Cardiac impulse, SAN",q:"The number of action potentials generated by sino-atrial node (SAN) in a healthy human is _______ per minute.",o:["70 \u2013 75","100 \u2013 110","120 - 140","28 - 30"],c:0,s:`The SAN in a healthy human generates 70 \u2013 75 action potentials per minute.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q122",pn:122,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood coagulation",q:"Which of the following statements is incorrect?",o:["Blood clot consists of fibrins","Fibrin is produced from fibrinogen","Fibrinogen is produced from fibrin","Blood coagulates in response to an injury"],c:2,s:`Fibrins are formed by the conversion of inactive fibrinogen in the plasma by the enzyme thrombin.
So fibrinogen is not produced from fibrin; that statement is incorrect.
Note: Question sits in the Botany block of the booklet but is Zoology content; sub set to Zoology.`},{id:"neet-2026r-q136",pn:136,y:"Re-NEET 2026 \xB7 21 Jun",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Human heart",q:"The opening between the right atrium and the right ventricle is guarded by _________.",o:["tricuspid valve","semilunar valve","sino-atrial node","bicuspid valve"],c:0,s:`The opening between the right atrium and right ventricle is guarded by a valve of three muscular flaps (cusps), the tricuspid valve.
A bicuspid (mitral) valve guards the opening between the left atrium and left ventricle.
The openings of the ventricles into the pulmonary artery and the aorta have semilunar valves.`},{id:"neet-2026-q155",pn:155,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood: WBC types",q:"The WBC count of a person's blood sample is 8000/cu mm. How many eosinophils and lymphocytes would be in the same blood sample approximately?",o:["160 \u2013 240/cu mm and 1600 \u2013 2000/cu mm respectively","100 \u2013 120/cu mm and 160 \u2013 200/cu mm respectively","300 \u2013 500/cu mm and 500 \u2013 700/cu mm respectively","300 \u2013 500/cu mm and 1200 \u2013 1500/cu mm respectively"],c:0,s:`Eosinophils are 2-3% of total WBCs. So the value is 2 to 3% of 8000/cu mm = 160 \u2013 240/cu mm.
Lymphocytes are 20-25% of total WBCs. So the value is about 20 to 25% of 8000/cu mm = 1600 \u2013 2000/cu mm.`},{id:"neet-2026-q167",pn:167,y:"NEET 2026 \xB7 3 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Rh grouping",q:`Select the incorrect statement with reference to Rh grouping.
A. Erythroblastosis foetalis is a condition observed having foetus with Rh\u207B\u1D5B\u1D49 blood and mother with Rh\u207A\u1D5B\u1D49 blood.
B. Rh antigen is observed on RBCs in the majority of human beings.
C. Before blood transfusion, Rh group should also be matched.
D. Rh incompatibility is observed when a pregnant mother is Rh\u207B\u1D5B\u1D49 and the foetus is Rh\u207A\u1D5B\u1D49.
E. Erythroblastosis foetalis can be avoided by administering anti-Rh antibodies to the mother immediately after the delivery of the second child.
Choose the answer from the options given below :`,o:["A and E only","A and B only","B and C only","C and D only"],c:0,s:`A is incorrect: the special Rh incompatibility occurs between an Rh\u207B\u1D5B\u1D49 pregnant mother and an Rh\u207A\u1D5B\u1D49 foetus, not the other way round.
B is correct: Rh antigen is on the surface of the RBCs of the majority (nearly 80 percent) of humans.
C is correct: Rh group must be matched before blood transfusion to avoid severe destruction of RBCs.
D is correct: Rh incompatibility (erythroblastosis foetalis) occurs when the pregnant mother is Rh\u207B\u1D5B\u1D49 and the foetus is Rh\u207A\u1D5B\u1D49.
E is incorrect: erythroblastosis foetalis can be avoided by giving anti-Rh antibodies to the mother immediately after the delivery of the first child.
Thus the incorrect statements are A and E.`},{id:"neet-2025-q112",pn:112,y:"NEET 2025",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Regulation of cardiac activity",q:`Cardiac activities of the heart are regulated by:
A. Nodal tissue
B. A special neural centre in the medulla oblongata
C. Adrenal medullary hormones
D. Adrenal cortical hormones
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A, B and C Only","A, B, C and D","A, C and D Only","A, B and D Only"],c:0,s:`Normal cardiac activity is regulated intrinsically, i.e., auto-regulated by specialised muscles (nodal tissue), so the heart is called myogenic.
A special neural centre in the medulla oblongata can moderate the cardiac function through the autonomic nervous system.
The sympathetic nervous system can increase the rate of heartbeat, ventricular contraction and thereby cardiac output.
Parasympathetic neural signals decrease the rate of heartbeat, the speed of conduction of action potential and thereby the cardiac output.
Adrenal medullary hormones can also increase the cardiac output.`},{id:"neet-2024-q166",pn:166,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Cardiac conduction pathway",q:`Following are the stages of pathway for conduction of an action potential through the heart :
A. AV bundle
B. Purkinje fibres
C. AV node
D. Bundle branches
E. SA node
Choose the correct sequence of pathway from the options given below :`,o:["B-D-E-C-A","E-A-D-B-C","E-C-A-D-B","A-E-C-B-D"],c:2,s:`Option (3) is correct.
The action potential travels: SA node \u2192 AV node \u2192 AV bundle \u2192 bundle branches \u2192 Purkinje fibres.`},{id:"neet-2024-q196",pn:196,y:"NEET 2024 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Electrocardiogram",q:`Match List I with List II:
A. P wave \u2014 I. Heart muscles are electrically silent.
B. QRS complex \u2014 II. Depolarisation of ventricles.
C. T wave \u2014 III. Depolarisation of atria.
D. T-P gap \u2014 IV. Repolarisation of ventricles.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below:`,o:["A-II, B-III, C-I, D-IV","A-IV, B-II, C-I, D-III","A-I, B-III, C-IV, D-II","A-III, B-II, C-IV, D-I"],c:3,s:`P wave \u2013 III: depolarisation of atria.
QRS complex \u2013 II: depolarisation of ventricles.
T wave \u2013 IV: repolarisation of ventricles.
T-P gap \u2013 I: heart muscles are electrically silent.`},{id:"neet-2023-q179",pn:179,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Electrocardiogram",q:`Math List I with List II.
List I: A. P-wave; B. Q-wave; C. QRS complex; D. T-wave
List II: I. Beginning of systole; II. Repolarisation of ventricles; III. Depolarisation of atria; IV. Depolarisation of ventricles
Choose the correct answer from the options given below :`,o:["A-I, B-II, C-III, D-IV","A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II","A-IV, B-III, C-II, D-I","A-II, B-IV, C-I, D-III"],c:1,s:`P-wave is depolarisation of the atria, which leads to atrial contraction.
Q-wave marks the beginning of systole.
QRS complex is depolarisation of the ventricles, which starts ventricular contraction.
T-wave is the return (repolarisation) of the ventricles to the normal state.
So A-III, B-I, C-IV, D-II.`},{id:"neet-2023-q189",pn:189,y:"NEET 2023 \xB7 7 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood: WBC types",q:`Which of the following statements are correct?
A. Basophils are most abundant cells of the total WBCs.
B. Basophils secrete histamine, serotonin and heparin.
C. Basophils are involved in inflammatory response.
D. Basophils have kidney shaped nucleus.
E. Basophils are agranulocytes.
Choose the correct answer from the options given below.`,o:["A and B only","D and E only","C and E only","B and C only"],c:3,s:`B and C are true: basophils secrete histamine, serotonin and heparin and take part in inflammation.
A is false: neutrophils are the most abundant (60-65%); basophils are the least (0.5-1%).
D is false: monocytes have a kidney-shaped nucleus.
E is false: basophils are granulocytes.`},{id:"neet-2022-q152",pn:152,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood clotting",q:`Given below are two statements :
Statement I : The coagulum is formed of network of threads called thrombins.
Statement II : Spleen is the graveyard of erythrocytes.
In the light of the above statements, choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["Both Statement I and Statement II are incorrect","Statement I is correct but Statement II is incorrect","Statement I is incorrect but Statement II is correct","Both Statement I and Statement II are correct"],c:2,s:`A clot (coagulum) is mainly a network of threads called fibrins, not thrombins.
So Statement I is incorrect.
RBCs are destroyed in the spleen, so it is called the graveyard of erythrocytes.
So Statement II is correct.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q180",pn:180,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Hepatic portal system",q:"A unique vascular connection between the digestive tract and liver is called",o:["Hepato-cystic system","Hepato-pancreatic system","Hepatic portal system","Renal portal system"],c:2,s:`The hepatic portal system is a special vein system.
It connects the digestive tract to the liver.`},{id:"neet-2022r-q188",pn:188,y:"Re-NEET 2022 \xB7 4 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood composition",q:`Arrange the following formed elements in the decreasing order of their abundance in blood in humans :
(a) Platelets
(b) Neutrophils
(c) Erythrocytes.
(d) Eosinophils
(e) Monocytes
Choose the most appropriate answer from the options given below :`,o:["(a), (c), (b), (d), (e)","(c), (a), (b), (e), (d)","(c), (b), (a), (e), (d)","(d), (e), (b), (a), (c)"],c:1,s:`RBCs: 5-5.5 million per mm\xB3.
Platelets: 1,50,000-3,50,000 per mm\xB3.
WBCs: 6,000-8,000 per mm\xB3.
Among WBCs: neutrophils 60-65%, monocytes 6-8%, eosinophils 2-3%.
Order: erythrocytes, platelets, neutrophils, monocytes, eosinophils.`},{id:"neet-2022-q199",pn:199,y:"NEET 2022 \xB7 17 Jul",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Cardiac cycle",q:"Which one of the following statements is correct?",o:["The tricuspid and the bicuspid valves open due to the pressure exerted by the simultaneous contraction of the atria.","Blood moves freely from atrium to the ventricle during joint diastole.","Increased ventricular pressure causes closing of the semilunar valves.","The atrio-ventricular node (AVN) generates an action potential to stimulate atrial contraction."],c:1,s:`During joint diastole the atrioventricular valves are open and blood flows freely from atria to ventricles, so (2) is correct.
Semilunar valves close when ventricular pressure falls, giving the 'dub' sound.
The SA node, not the AVN, starts the action potential for atrial contraction.
AV valves open because of blood pressure in the atria and falling pressure in the ventricles.`},{id:"neet-2021-q161",pn:161,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood groups",q:`Persons with 'AB' blood group are called as "Universal recipients". This is due to :`,o:["Absence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B, in plasma","Absence of antigens A and B on the surface of RBCs","Absence of antigens A and B in plasma","Presence of antibodies, anti-A and anti-B, on RBCs"],c:0,s:`AB persons have both antigens A and B on their red cells.
Their plasma has neither anti-A nor anti-B, so no antibody attacks donor cells of any group.
That is why they are universal recipients.`},{id:"neet-2021-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2021 \xB7 12 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood clotting",q:"Which enzyme is responsible for the conversion of inactive fibrinogens to fibrins?",o:["Thrombokinase","Thrombin","Renin","Epinephrine"],c:1,s:`Thrombokinase (enzyme complex) converts prothrombin to thrombin.
Thrombin then converts fibrinogen to fibrin threads, which form the clot.
Renin and epinephrine have other roles.`},{id:"neet-2020-q009",pn:9,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"ECG",q:"The QRS complex in a standard ECG represents :",o:["Depolarisation of auricles","Depolarisation of ventricles","Repolarisation of ventricles","Repolarisation of auricles"],c:1,s:`In an ECG the P wave is atrial depolarisation.
The QRS complex is ventricular depolarisation.
The T wave is ventricular repolarisation.`},{id:"neet-2020-q078",pn:78,y:"NEET 2020 \xB7 13 Sep",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Blood cells",q:`Match the following columns and select the correct option.
(a) Eosinophils  (i) Immune response
(b) Basophils  (ii) Phagocytosis
(c) Neutrophils  (iii) Release histaminase, destructive enzymes
(d) Lymphocytes  (iv) Release granules containing histamine`,o:["(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(i), (b)-(ii), (c)-(iv), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(iii), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iii), (b)-(iv), (c)-(ii), (d)-(i)"],c:3,s:`Eosinophils release histaminase and destructive enzymes (iii).
Basophils release granules with histamine (iv).
Neutrophils do phagocytosis (ii).
Lymphocytes give immune response (i).`},{id:"neet-2019-q026",pn:26,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Cardiac output",q:"What would be the heart rate of a person if the cardiac output is 5L, blood volume in the ventricles at the end of diastole is 100 mL and at the end of ventricular systole is 50 mL ?",o:["100 beats per minute","125 beats per minute","50 beats per minute","75 beats per minute"],c:0,s:`Stroke volume = 100 \u2212 50 = 50 mL.
Cardiac output = stroke volume \xD7 heart rate.
5000 mL = 50 mL \xD7 heart rate.
Heart rate = 100 beats per minute.`},{id:"neet-2019-q066",pn:66,y:"NEET 2019 \xB7 5 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Electrocardiogram",q:`Match the Column-I with Column-II :
Column-I: (a) P-wave (b) QRS complex (c) T-wave (d) Reduction in the size of T-wave
Column-II: (i) Depolarisation of ventricles (ii) Repolarisation of ventricles (iii) Coronary ischemia (iv) Depolarisation of atria (v) Repolarisation of atria
Select the correct option.`,o:["(a)-(ii), (b)-(i), (c)-(v), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(ii), (b)-(iii), (c)-(v), (d)-(iv)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii)","(a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(v)"],c:2,s:`P-wave is depolarisation of atria.
QRS complex is depolarisation of ventricles.
T-wave is repolarisation of ventricles.
A smaller T-wave points to coronary ischemia.
So (a)-(iv), (b)-(i), (c)-(ii), (d)-(iii).`},{id:"neet-2018-q170",pn:170,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Heart valves",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II. Column I: a. Tricuspid valve, b. Bicuspid valve, c. Semilunar valve. Column II: i. Between left atrium and left ventricle, ii. Between right ventricle and pulmonary artery, iii. Between right atrium and right ventricle",o:["a-iii, b-i, c-ii","a-i, b-iii, c-ii","a-i, b-ii, c-iii","a-ii, b-i, c-iii"],c:0,s:`The tricuspid valve guards the right atrium and right ventricle opening: a\u2013iii.
The bicuspid (mitral) valve is between left atrium and left ventricle: b\u2013i.
Semilunar valves are at the base of the pulmonary artery and aorta: c\u2013ii.`},{id:"neet-2018-q178",pn:178,y:"NEET 2018 \xB7 6 May",tp:"Body Fluids and Circulation",st:"Plasma proteins",q:"Match the items given in Column I with those in Column II. Column I: a. Fibrinogen, b. Globulin, c. Albumin. Column II: i. Osmotic balance, ii. Blood clotting, iii. Defence mechanism",o:["a-iii, b-ii, c-i","a-i, b-ii, c-iii","a-i, b-iii, c-ii","a-ii, b-iii, c-i"],c:3,s:`Fibrinogen takes part in blood clotting: a\u2013ii.
Globulins (including antibodies) are for defence: b\u2013iii.
Albumin maintains osmotic balance: c\u2013i.`}]})();
